Here is a complete, end-to-end solution that runs SymmetricDS directly on your 4 database VPS machines (region-1 through region-4) and uses Wintersys's native PutToDatastore.sh, SyncFromDatastore.sh, and DeleteFromDatastore.sh scripts to bridge your MySQL and PostgreSQL databases across all 4 regions—with zero rclone dependencies and without touching the heavyweight filesystem syncer.

How the Native Datastore Bridge Works

Wintersys's datastore architecture supports chaining multiple regional S3 buckets using pipe-delimited (|) credentials in your deployment template (e.g., S3_HOST_BASE="ams3.digitaloceanspaces.com|fra1.digitaloceanspaces.com|...").
• PutToDatastore.sh in "distributed" mode automatically pushes a batch file to all chained regional S3 buckets in one call (so each region reads from its own local S3 bucket with zero cross-region GET latency).
• SyncFromDatastore.sh in "local" mode pulls incoming batches exclusively from that region's local S3 bucket into SymmetricDS's inbound queue.
• Native DB Connection Helpers: You can use Wintersys's ${HOME}/utilities/remote/ConnectToMySQLDB.sh and ${HOME}/utilities/remote/ConnectToPostgresDB.sh to apply the initial primary-key and SymmetricDS metadata setup. [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Deployment/ConnectingToCurrentDatabase)

Step 1: Configure Multi-Region S3 Chaining in Your Templates

In each region's Wintersys deployment template, configure S3_ACCESS_KEY, S3_SECRET_KEY, S3_HOST_BASE, and S3_LOCATION with that region's local S3 bucket first, followed by the other 3 regions:
bash
# Example for Region 1's template (Region 1 S3 endpoint MUST be first)
export S3_ACCESS_KEY="<R1_KEY>|<R2_KEY>|<R3_KEY>|<R4_KEY>"
export S3_SECRET_KEY="<R1_SECRET>|<R2_SECRET>|<R3_SECRET>|<R4_SECRET>"
export S3_HOST_BASE="<R1_S3_HOST>|<R2_S3_HOST>|<R3_S3_HOST>|<R4_S3_HOST>"
export S3_LOCATION="US|US|US|US"
Use code with caution.
(Repeat for Regions 2, 3, and 4, rotating the list so each region's local S3 endpoint sits at position 1.)

Step 2: Prevent Primary Key Collisions Across All 4 Databases

Before starting replication, stagger primary key generation on each database VPS so two regions never mint the same ID:
• On MySQL / MariaDB Regions (connect via ${HOME}/utilities/remote/ConnectToMySQLDB.sh):sql
-- Set offset = 1 for region-1, 2 for region-2, 3 for region-3, 4 for region-4
SET PERSIST auto_increment_increment = 10;
SET PERSIST auto_increment_offset = 1;
Use code with caution.
• On PostgreSQL Regions (connect via ${HOME}/utilities/remote/ConnectToPostgresDB.sh):sql
-- Run for each auto-incrementing sequence; adjust RESTART WITH (1, 2, 3, or 4) per region
ALTER SEQUENCE your_table_id_seq INCREMENT BY 10 RESTART WITH 1;
Use code with caution.
(Skip this if your tables already use UUID primary keys.) [1] (https://www.wintersys-initiatives.uk/Agile%20Deployment%20Toolkit/Deployment/ConnectingToCurrentDatabase)

Step 3: Install & Configure SymmetricDS on Each Database VPS

1. Install Java 17 and SymmetricDS on all 4 database VPS machines:bash
apt-get update && apt-get install -y openjdk-17-jre-headless unzip wget
wget -q https://sourceforge.net
unzip -q symmetric-server-3.15.10.zip -d /opt/
mv /opt/symmetric-server-* /opt/symmetric-ds
mkdir -p /opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/{outgoing,incoming}
Use code with caution.
2. Cap JVM Memory in /opt/symmetric-ds/conf/sym_service.conf so Java never competes with MySQL/Postgres for RAM:properties
wrapper.java.initmemory=128
wrapper.java.maxmemory=384
Use code with caution.
3. Create the Engine Properties File (/opt/symmetric-ds/engines/region-1.properties — adjust region-X and JDBC URL per VPS):For a MySQL Database VPS:properties
engine.name=region-1
group.id=mesh-node
external.id=region-1

db.driver=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver
db.url=jdbc:mysql://127.0.0.1:3306/YOUR_DB_NAME?useSSL=false&allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true&tinyInt1isBit=false
db.user=YOUR_DB_USER
db.password=YOUR_DB_PASSWORD

registration.url=
sync.url=file:///opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge
transport.type=file
offline.outbound.dir=/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/outgoing
offline.inbound.dir=/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/incoming

job.push.period.time.ms=10000
job.pull.period.time.ms=10000
job.routing.period.time.ms=5000
Use code with caution.
For a PostgreSQL Database VPS:properties
engine.name=region-2
group.id=mesh-node
external.id=region-2

db.driver=org.postgresql.Driver
db.url=jdbc:postgresql://127.0.0.1:5432/YOUR_DB_NAME?sslmode=disable
db.user=YOUR_DB_USER
db.password=YOUR_DB_PASSWORD

registration.url=
sync.url=file:///opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge
transport.type=file
offline.outbound.dir=/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/outgoing
offline.inbound.dir=/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/incoming

job.push.period.time.ms=10000
job.pull.period.time.ms=10000
job.routing.period.time.ms=5000
Use code with caution.

Step 4: Create the Native Wintersys Datastore Bridge Script

Create /usr/local/bin/wintersys-db-bridge.sh on each database VPS. This script mounts the shared bucket (<website_url>-db-sync-bridge), pushes completed outgoing batches to all regions via PutToDatastore.sh ("distributed"), and pulls only batches addressed to the local node (*-to-${NODE_ID}-*) via GetFromDatastore.sh ("local"):
bash
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

# --- CONFIGURATION (Set NODE_ID to region-1, region-2, region-3, or region-4) ---
NODE_ID="region-1"
HOME_DIR="/root"
DS_OPS="${HOME_DIR}/services/datastore/operations"
WEBSITE_URL="$("${HOME_DIR}/utilities/config/ExtractConfigValue.sh" 'WEBSITEURL')"
BUCKET="${WEBSITE_URL}-db-sync-bridge"

OUT_DIR="/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/outgoing"
IN_DIR="/opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/incoming"
LOCK_FILE="/tmp/wintersys-db-bridge.lock"

# Prevent overlapping runs
exec 200>"${LOCK_FILE}"
flock -n 200 || exit 0

# Ensure the sync bucket exists across all chained regions
"${DS_OPS}/MountDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET}" "distributed" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

# 1. PUSH OUTGOING BATCHES (Ignore .tmp files still being written by SymmetricDS)
for filepath in "${OUT_DIR}"/*; do
    [ -e "${filepath}" ] || continue
    filename="$(basename "${filepath}")"
    [[ "${filename}" == *.tmp ]] && continue

    # SymmetricDS names offline batch files: <source>-to-<target>-<batch_id>.csv
    # Extract target node ID from filename
    target_node="$(echo "${filename}" | sed -n 's/.*-to-\(region-[0-9]\+\)-.*/\1/p')"
    [ -z "${target_node}" ] && continue

    # Push to the target region's folder across all chained datastores and delete local file on success
    "${DS_OPS}/PutToDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET}" "${filepath}" "${target_node}" "distributed" "yes"
done

# 2. PULL INCOMING BATCHES FOR THIS REGION FROM LOCAL DATASTORE
# List objects in s3://<bucket>/<NODE_ID>/
mapfile -t incoming_files < <("${DS_OPS}/ListFromDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET}" "${NODE_ID}" "local" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $NF}' | grep -E '\.csv$' || true)

for remote_path in "${incoming_files[@]}"; do
    [ -z "${remote_path}" ] && continue
    filename="$(basename "${remote_path}")"
    local_target="${IN_DIR}/${filename}"
    local_tmp="${IN_DIR}/.${filename}.tmp"

    # Download atomically via .tmp file so SymmetricDS doesn't read a half-downloaded CSV
    "${DS_OPS}/GetFromDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET}/${NODE_ID}/${filename}" "${local_tmp}" "local"
    if [ -s "${local_tmp}" ]; then
        mv "${local_tmp}" "${local_target}"
        # Remove processed batch from S3 across all chained buckets so it isn't re-applied
        "${DS_OPS}/DeleteFromDatastore.sh" "${BUCKET}" "${NODE_ID}/${filename}" "distributed"
    fi
done
Use code with caution.
Make it executable and schedule it via a systemd timer (or cron loop every 10 seconds):
bash
chmod +x /usr/local/bin/wintersys-db-bridge.sh

# Add to root crontab (runs every 10 seconds)
(crontab -l 2>/dev/null; echo "* * * * * for i in 0 10 20 30 40 50; do (sleep \$i; /usr/local/bin/wintersys-db-bridge.sh) & done") | crontab -
Use code with caution.

Step 5: Initialize SymmetricDS & Load the 4-Region Mesh Rules

1. Start SymmetricDS on Region 1 first to generate the sym_* system tables in Region 1's database:bash
/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service install
/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service start
Use code with caution.
Wait ~15 seconds for the sym_* tables to be created, then run this SQL configuration on Region 1 only:sql
-- 1. Define the peer group
INSERT INTO sym_node_group (node_group_id) VALUES ('mesh-node');

-- 2. Enable bidirectional full-mesh syncing ('W' = wait for pull / offline batch)
INSERT INTO sym_node_group_link (source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, data_event_action)
VALUES ('mesh-node', 'mesh-node', 'W');

-- 3. Create replication channel with moderate batch size to keep DB VPS CPU low
INSERT INTO sym_channel (channel_id, processing_order, max_batch_size, enabled)
VALUES ('default_channel', 1, 500, 1);

-- 4. Capture changes on all user tables; sync_on_incoming_batch=0 prevents echo loops
INSERT INTO sym_trigger (trigger_id, source_table_name, channel_id, sync_on_incoming_batch, last_update_time, create_time)
VALUES ('all_tables', '*', 'default_channel', 0, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);

-- 5. Route all changes to all other nodes in the mesh
INSERT INTO sym_router (router_id, source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, router_type, create_time, last_update_time)
VALUES ('mesh_router', 'mesh-node', 'mesh-node', 'default', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);

INSERT INTO sym_trigger_router (trigger_id, router_id, initial_load_order, create_time, last_update_time)
VALUES ('all_tables', 'mesh_router', 100, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);

-- 6. Conflict resolution: newest timestamp wins across regions
INSERT INTO sym_conflict (conflict_id, source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, detect_type, resolve_type, ping_back, create_time, last_update_time)
VALUES ('newest_wins_rule', 'mesh-node', 'mesh-node', 'USE_TIMESTAMP', 'NEWER_WINS', 'OFF', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
Use code with caution.
2. Start SymmetricDS on Regions 2, 3, and 4:bash
/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service install
/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service start
Use code with caution.
Region 1 will push its sym_* routing metadata into the S3 bridge, and Regions 2, 3, and 4 will automatically pull it down, build triggers on their local MySQL/PostgreSQL tables, and begin multi-directional replication.

Operational Checklist

• Persistent across rebuilds: Drop the SymmetricDS installation, region-X.properties, and /usr/local/bin/wintersys-db-bridge.sh into your Wintersys database post-build customization hook so any rebuilt DB VPS rejoins automatically.
• Verify sync health: Check /opt/symmetric-ds/logs/symmetric.log or query SELECT * FROM sym_outgoing_batch WHERE status != 'OK'; on any database to confirm batches are clearing cleanly.
If you want to customize this further, let me know:
• Whether your tables already have a UTC updated_at timestamp column (needed for USE_TIMESTAMP conflict resolution) or if you prefer primary-key fallback resolution
• If you also want a Wintersys post-build hook snippet to automate installing this whenever a new DB machine is spun up

Each region discovers updates through a two-stage polling loop—first at the S3 bucket level, and then at the local filesystem level—using the batch filename as the routing address.
Here is the exact step-by-step lifecycle of an update from Region 1 to Region 2:

1. Outgoing Batch Creation (Region 1)

1. Trigger fires: A row is inserted or updated in Region 1's database. SymmetricDS's database trigger writes the change to its internal sym_data table.
2. Batch packaging: Every 5–10 seconds (job.push.period.time.ms), SymmetricDS's offline transport packages those changes into a CSV file in /opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/outgoing/ with the target node baked right into the filename:
region-1-to-region-2-000042.csv (along with matching files for -to-region-3- and -to-region-4-).

2. S3 Upload & Folder Routing (Region 1 Bridge Script)

3. Filename inspection: Every 10 seconds, /usr/local/bin/wintersys-db-bridge.sh scans /opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/outgoing/.
4. Targeted upload: It parses -to-region-2- from the filename and calls PutToDatastore.sh to upload that specific file into the region-2/ prefix inside the shared S3 bucket:
s3://<bucket>/region-2/region-1-to-region-2-000042.csv
5. Local cleanup: Once PutToDatastore.sh confirms the upload succeeded, it deletes the local outgoing file so it isn't uploaded twice.

3. S3 Polling & Download (Region 2 Bridge Script)

6. Mailbox check: Every 10 seconds, Region 2's wintersys-db-bridge.sh runs ListFromDatastore.sh exclusively on its own S3 "inbox" prefix (s3://<bucket>/region-2/).
7. Atomic download: Because a file is sitting in region-2/, Region 2 knows immediately it has an update waiting. It downloads the file to a hidden .tmp file inside /opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/incoming/ and renames it to region-1-to-region-2-000042.csv once the download completes.
8. S3 cleanup: Immediately after the rename succeeds, Region 2 calls DeleteFromDatastore.sh to remove region-1-to-region-2-000042.csv from S3 so it won't download it again on the next 10-second tick.

4. Local Ingestion & Application (Region 2 SymmetricDS)

9. Local directory poll: Every 10 seconds (job.pull.period.time.ms), Region 2's SymmetricDS background worker checks /opt/symmetric-ds/s3-bridge/incoming/.
10. Apply & delete: Seeing region-1-to-region-2-000042.csv, it parses the CSV, applies the SQL INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE statements to Region 2's local database (with sync_on_incoming_batch=0 so Region 2's triggers ignore it), logs the batch ID in sym_incoming_batch to guarantee idempotency, and deletes the local CSV file.

Summary of the "Notification" Mechanism

There are no push webhooks or open network ports between servers. Instead:
• S3 prefixes (region-1/, region-2/, region-3/, region-4/) act as dedicated per-region mailboxes.
• 10-second cron polling (ListFromDatastore.sh + SymmetricDS's job.pull.period.time.ms) acts as the heartbeat that checks the mailbox, giving you an end-to-end replication latency of roughly 10 to 25 seconds across regions.
If you'd like to dive deeper into failure handling or edge cases, let me know:
• How you'd like to handle offline regions (e.g., if Region 3 goes down for a day and accumulates thousands of files in its S3 inbox)
• Whether you want to add monitoring/alerting if a batch fails to apply due to a schema mismatch or constraint error


Your actual application data updated by SymmetricDS should definitely be stored in your database backups and baselines, while SymmetricDS's internal sym_* system tables should be excluded.
In a multi-region mesh, any data replicated from Region 2 into Region 1 is genuine production data—so your regular database backups must capture it. However, backing up SymmetricDS's internal sym_* tables (such as sym_data, sym_outgoing_batch, and sym_incoming_batch) into a baseline image can break replication if you ever restore or spin up a new server from that baseline.

Why sym_* Tables Must Be Excluded From Baselines & Backups

• Stale Node Identity & Batch Cursors: sym_node_identity stores the specific region-X ID of the server that took the backup. If you spin up a new Region 4 server using a baseline taken from Region 1, the new server will wake up thinking it is region-1, causing split-brain conflicts and duplicate batch IDs.
• Bloated Backup Sizes: sym_data and sym_data_event log every single row change over time. Including them in hourly or daily Wintersys database backups inflates archive sizes and slows down restore times.
• Broken Triggers on Restore: Database dumps include the sym_on_i_*, sym_on_u_*, and sym_on_d_* triggers attached to your user tables. If you restore a dump onto a fresh server before SymmetricDS is configured, those triggers will fire on every INSERT during the restore and either fail or flood sym_data with millions of redundant events.

How to Configure Wintersys Backups & Baselines Properly


1. Exclude sym_* Tables From Wintersys Scheduled Backups

In Wintersys's database backup scripts (which run mysqldump or pg_dump before pushing to S3 via PutToDatastore.sh), pass ignore flags so only your application tables are backed up:
• For MySQL / MariaDB (mysqldump):
Pass --ignore-table for the sym_* metadata tables (or dynamically exclude all tables matching sym_%):bash
# Generate --ignore-table flags for all sym_* tables dynamically
IGNORE_TABLES=$(mysql -N -B -e "SELECT CONCAT('--ignore-table=', table_schema, '.', table_name) FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = '${DB_NAME}' AND table_name LIKE 'sym_%';")

mysqldump ${IGNORE_TABLES} --single-transaction "${DB_NAME}" > backup.sql
Use code with caution.
• For PostgreSQL (pg_dump):
PostgreSQL natively supports wildcards for excluding tables and triggers:bash
pg_dump --exclude-table='sym_*' --exclude-table-data='sym_*' "${DB_NAME}" > backup.sql
Use code with caution.

2. Keep Baselines Clean (Application Schema + Seed Data Only)

When creating a Wintersys Baseline (used to spin up fresh deployments or replace a destroyed database VPS):
• Never include sym_* tables or sym_* triggers in your baseline SQL dump.
• Let SymmetricDS rebuild them on boot: When a newly spun-up database VPS restores a clean baseline and starts sym_service, SymmetricDS automatically detects that sym_* tables are missing, recreates them cleanly for that specific region-X identity, attaches fresh triggers, and pulls any missed updates from the mesh.

Summary Rule of Thumb


Data Type	Include in Regular Backups?	Include in Wintersys Baseline?
User Tables (including rows synced from other regions)	Yes	Yes (seed/initial state)
SymmetricDS sym_* Tables & Triggers	No (exclude sym_*)	No (auto-generated on boot)

If you'd like to automate this inside your Wintersys setup, let me know:
• Whether you want the exact hook inside Wintersys's backup script (BackupDB.sh) to strip sym_* tables automatically
• How you plan to re-sync a replaced database VPS (e.g., pulling the latest clean backup from S3 before starting sym_service)



